In article <01bc5644$ba2ff040$aa1f29a6@con370.con.mci.com>, "foobar" <foo@bar> writes:
|This is provided in DECnet/OSI (or DECnet-Plus as they now call it). It is
|called
|RFC 1006 and is configured at the OSI TRANSPORT layer in NET$CONFIGURE.COM

The above is one of several methods to allow DECnet apps to work over
a TCP/IP network. In more detail, the options are:

1. DECnet/OSI over TCP/IP.  Requires DECnet-Plus (DECnet/OSI, DECnetPhaseV)
   on all systems.  Requires a TCP/IP stack.

2. DECnetIV encapsulated in TCP/IP. 
   a) using VAX/VMS gateways as tunnel endpoints:
      - VAX/VMS gateways must have DECnetIV routing licenses
      - VAX/VMS gateways must have TCP/IP stacks that support it
        (MultiNet or I understand TCPware or Pathway - NOT UCX)
      - will allow DECnetIV-only nodes to communicate thru tunnel
   b) using Cisco IOS routers as tunnel endpoints:
      - both ends of tunnel must be IOS routers
      - will allow DECnetIV-only nodes to communicate thru tunnel

3. Phase/IP
   - provides DECnetIV API over native TCP/IP
   - MultiNet only
   - does not require any DECnet

Given the config described below - MultiNet on one end of the IP
cloud and UCX on the other - you can't get there from here.
You could replace the UCX with MultiNet and then either run
Phase/IP between the boxes or tunnel DECnetIV in IP.  And/or
you could put DECnet/OSI on each box and run DECnet/OSI over TCP/IP.
Or you could buy Cisco routers for each side of the cloud and use
them to tunnel DECnetIV in IP - we'd like that just fine ;-).

Cheers,

Aaron

